#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Switch a fixed set of privacy protections for stock Omarchy on and off,
and report whether each one is still in place, for the
brightwalker25.black-ops bar panel and for use from a terminal.

Design notes:

- Black Ops covers what stock Omarchy ships and sets up. Leaks that belong
  to an app you install yourself are that app's to handle, and rows for them
  were removed in 0.2.0 without undoing them (see FORMER_ROWS).
- The script decides and the panel renders. Whether a protection is in
  place, whether it has slipped, and what switching it would do are worked
  out here and published as JSON. The QML holds no policy, and every answer
  it shows can be reproduced at the command line with `black-ops --text`.
- Nothing here touches the network. A status run reads a handful of local
  files and nothing else. It is cheap enough to run on a timer with the
  panel closed, which is what lets the bar icon turn amber on its own.
- Nothing here runs as root. The changes that need root (the hosts block
  and the drop-ins in /etc) are made by a separate helper that
  the installer copies to /usr/local/lib and that sudo lets this account run
  without a password, with one fixed verb and nothing else. Until the
  installer has been run, or while the installed copy is older than the one
  in the plugin folder, those rows say so and the helper is not called.
- Switching off undoes only what this plugin applied or adopted. A small
  state file records which rows those are. A protection that was already in
  place when the plugin was switched on is adopted: it is recorded, not
  applied a second time, and it is removed on the way off like any other,
  because off means the machine as it ships.
- Colour is decided here too, by one rule. Green is on and holding. Amber
  is a real problem: a protection that is switched on but not in place, the
  helper needs reinstalling, a change waits for a reconnect or a new login,
  a row cannot be verified, a pair window has overrun, or something could
  not be removed. Red is kept for one thing only: listen mode saw a program
  on this machine actually contact a host that matches a telemetry
  signature. Grey is off, switched off by you, or not applicable. An
  optional row that is off is grey, never amber.
- Optional rows are off by default, have their own switch, and are never
  switched on by the main switch.
- Rows that other stacks register in rows.d only report. They have no
  switch, because switchable fixes cover stock Omarchy only.
- It sends nothing anywhere and logs nothing. The state file holds row
  names, the words "applied" or "adopted", times, short error messages, the
  Omarchy version last checked, and what a few rows need to put things back
  exactly: the Bluetooth alias it replaced, the update badge's place on the
  bar, and which developer tools it changed. It never holds a coordinate or
  a file's contents.

Exit status is 0 whenever a status was printed, including after an action
that partly failed; the failures are reported inside the JSON.
"""
import argparse
import contextlib
import fcntl
import hashlib
import json
import math
import os
